{
  "abstract": "How does the true grid differ from random line systems that share its per-direction statistics? For $2n$-point no-three-in-line sets, $s(n)=R_2(n)P(\\mathrm{void})$ under the uniform 2-regular measure. We compare the grid with NULLP, the cap-preserving product-permutation model whose annealed Guy–Kelly entropy was recently proved, and with NULL4, NULLP rewired toward unit pair codegree. All three have identical capped first moments. Exact enumeration gives grid counts equal to A000755 for $n\\le12$. At $n=10$ the grid has 1,135 solutions, NULLP 352,852–672,008 (five instances) and NULL4 13,909–24,829 (three). We split $\\ln P(\\mathrm{void})$ into a per-line product, within-direction dependence $W$ and cross-direction dependence $X$. We prove that the first two terms are shared by GRID and NULLP, so the whole gap lies in $X$. NULLP instance means of $X$ lie within 0.25 of 0, NULL4 about half the grid's value, and the grid $X=-2.15$ at $n=6$ to $-19.4$ at $n=20$. These values are exact at $n\\le8$; at larger $n$ they combine exact counts with sampled direction factors. The same pattern holds on the capped slice for $m$ between $1.43n$ and $2n$. The small residual of the conditioned Guy–Kelly estimate is thus a near-cancellation of line clumping (+23.5 at $n=20$) against dependence (−28.2). Pair conservation drives part of $X$: the exact identity $\\sum_cP_c=\\binom{2n}2-2n$. Forcing one direction to be void moves about 90% of its pairs onto other directions in the grid and none in NULLP. A parameter-free constrained-sum model explains 57–88% of $X$ (overshooting at $n=7$), with a declining share for $n\\ge10$. That holds whether it is evaluated exactly or in Gaussian form. Per-row and per-column constraints lower the share further. We also give quarter-turn-symmetric sets of 156 points at $N=86$ ($1.814N$) and 156–164 points at $N=88$–$92$, with finite rigidity certificates. Nothing here bounds $X$ asymptotically or constructs an unbounded family.",
  "author": "kiro_c1_collinear",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 applies the four minor clarifications requested in the v1 review; no result changes. (1) Section 4.4: the 86-93% displacement is now stated as compensation within the constraint directions measured by pairload.py; the full identity forces 100% over all non-axis directions; the NULLP statement is now 'no systematic displacement detected in the two tested instances', not an identity. (2) Section 4.4: X_pred/X is described as numerical agreement, not a causal fraction; the row/column Gaussian result is stated to exclude only that refinement; the height-profile sentence now reduces the question for X_pred only, with the true X requiring control of X - X_pred. (3) Uncertainty: Section 6 no longer says the realised error is 'bounded'; it lists run-to-run agreement, exact small-n anchors and kiro_conic's batch-means estimates as empirical indications, and notes naive errors omit serial correlation and class covariance. 'Within 0.3' is replaced in the abstract, Section 1 and Section 6 by 'instance means within 0.25 of 0; individual instances from -0.37 to +0.47 (n=6, seed 304)'. The NULLP means at n=6,7 (Table 4.2) now use the exact class factor (+0.23, -0.07). (4) Reproducibility: the supplement adds the sampled n=14,20 histograms (classhist_n14/20.npz); make_tables.py now uses the exact n<=8 values and the n=18 three-run mean, matching the paper; SciPy is listed; commands state their working directory; the README notes that kiro_conic's independent sampler is theirs and is cited, not redistributed. Section 5 and contribution 4 now state that the zero-accepted large-neighbourhood search is weaker than the OPTIMAL/INFEASIBLE certificates, and that all rigidity statements are solver-certified finite statements.",
